# The table of `cl100k_base`, the encoding of GPT-4, GPT-3.5 and the
# embedding models `text-embedding-ada-002` and `text-embedding-3-*`, alone: a
# program that uses this module carries this table and no other, where one
# that uses `tokenizer/tables`, which also
# finds an encoding by its name or its model, carries all three. Measured
# 2026-11-18 on this Mac at -O2, a program that counts two words is 2.05 MB
# with `tokenizer/tables/cl100k_base`, 3.42 MB with
# `tokenizer/tables/o200k_base`, 4.97 MB through `tokenizer/tables`, and
# 0.81 MB with no table. What an encoding does is `tokenizer`'s. Nothing
# here fails.

use tokenizer
use tokenizer/tables/internal/cl100k_base as cl100k
use tokenizer/tables/internal/made as making

# The encoding `cl100k_base`: 100,256 ordinary tokens and the special tokens
# `<|endoftext|>` (100257), `<|fim_prefix|>`, `<|fim_middle|>`, `<|fim_suffix|>`
# and `<|endofprompt|>` (100276).
#
# It reads the whole table; make it once and keep it. See also
# `tokenizer.encode_ordinary`.
function encoding() -> tokenizer.Encoding
    return making.made(
        name: "cl100k_base",
        pattern: cl100k.PATTERN,
        ranks: cl100k.RANKS,
        texts: cl100k.SPECIAL_TEXTS,
        numbers: cl100k.SPECIAL_TOKENS
    )

## Examples

# Two words counted and encoded in `cl100k_base`.
test "encoding: two words"
    enc = encoding()
    assert enc.name == "cl100k_base"
    assert tokenizer.encode_ordinary(enc, text: "hello world") == [15339, 1917]
